Emitting brotli streams from slz: thoughts and measurements
==========================================================

Idea first discussed on 2026-11-01 (discussion only, no code), resumed
on 2026-11-08 with the first measurements. See also ../encoding/README for the
measurement tools (SLZ_STATS, SLZ_DRY_RUN, FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH) and for
the cost split of the current encoder.


1. The idea
-----------

What costs both CPU and ratio in slz is suspected to be deflate's
bitstream layout, which is old and inefficient. Brotli (RFC 7932) is
more modern. Could a variant of slz emit a brotli-compatible stream?
The goal is NOT to beat brotli's speed, but to keep slz's current speed
and its zero-memory, stateless design (millions of streams in haproxy),
and maybe gain some ratio from a better designed format. It would
compress less than brotli's high levels, but possibly about as well as
q0/q1 without their memory usage.

Motivation at the time: lzbench showed brotli 1.2.0 -0 compressing at
the speed of slz 1.2 (i.e. 66% of current slz) to 37% where slz gave
41%. That was a whole-file measurement, see section 4 for why it does
not reflect haproxy's regime.

Constraints: browsers only accept "br" over HTTPS (most traffic today).
The window must only cover the current call, which the stream header's
WBITS allows (16 to 24 bits); each call ends on a meta-block boundary
and the decoder never sees references to earlier calls. haproxy knows
its buffer size and could declare a 16 or 32 kB window.


2. The format, from a stateless encoder's point of view
-------------------------------------------------------

Brotli is LZ77 + Huffman like deflate, but:
  - meta-blocks, each with block types and block switch commands;
  - separate prefix codes for literals, insert-and-copy lengths and
    distances;
  - commands: one insert-and-copy symbol combines the literal count and
    the match length, followed by the literals and a distance;
  - a ring of the last 4 distances, with short codes for them and for
    small variations around the last two;
  - context modeling: literals coded with one of up to 256 trees picked
    from the two previous bytes; distance trees picked by copy length;
  - a static dictionary of 122,784 bytes of words and phrases from web
    content (English, HTML, JS, CSS, other languages) with 121 transforms
    (uppercase first, add a space or '">', etc.), referenced as copies
    whose distance lies beyond the window;
  - no checksum.

What would help a stateless encoder:
  - combined insert-and-copy commands: one symbol per match instead of
    deflate's length code + extra bits, nothing per literal run. Fewer
    operations and branches. (Note: the insert length is then paid in
    the command's code and extra bits, while deflate has no per-run
    marker, so the bit saving is not obvious; the CPU saving is.)
  - the distance cache: "same distance as last time" or one of the last
    4 with +-1..3 costs a short code with no extra bits, and insert-and-
    copy codes 0-127 even imply the last distance at no cost (insert
    0-9, copy 2-69). Measured in section 5;
  - cheap framing: an empty metadata block (~1 byte) byte-aligns the
    stream, vs deflate's 5-byte empty stored block on flush; a stored
    meta-block costs 3-5 bytes for up to 16 MB vs 5 bytes per 64 kB;
  - no checksum: only matters for haproxy's gzip output (CRC32 over every
    byte) and zlib's Adler-32; raw deflate has none, so it changes nothing
    in zenc/lzbench measurements;
  - variable-length distances as in deflate (code + extra bits growing
    with log2 of the distance), with the window declared once.

What is less favourable:
  - no fixed huffman tree: each compressed meta-block must describe its
    prefix codes. Same approach as the text tree: train the code
    descriptions offline on web content and emit them as a constant bit
    blob per profile (text, generic), only when opening a block that the
    known savings repay;
  - literals cost the same as in deflate (one huffman code each), unless
    using context modeling, which means several literal trees (bigger
    constant header) and a branch per literal on the previous byte's
    class. If tried: 2 or 4 trees selected by the previous byte's class;
  - the insert-and-copy table (704 codes, insert and copy ranges, split
    at code 128 for the implicit distance) is more complex than deflate's
    length table, but reduces to a couple of small lookups per match;
  - the match finder and hashing, which dominate slz's time, stay the
    same: SLZ_DRY_RUN showed that encoding is only 16-19% of the time,
    two thirds of it for literals (../encoding/README, section 2). So a brotli
    emitter can at best keep today's speed, as intended, not improve it
    much.

Brotli q0/q1 for comparison: they don't need much memory either (small
hash tables; the MBs come from the streaming ring buffer and the q >= 2
hashers). What they do per fragment is build huffman codes from the
data's statistics (sampled histogram for literals, two passes for
commands at q1), which costs CPU we wouldn't spend with static codes.
They don't use the static dictionary.


3. The dictionary subset
------------------------

The decoder has the whole 122,784-byte dictionary, but the encoder only
needs the words it chooses to reference, so it does not have to embed it
all (which would considerably inflate our code size). A reference is
(word length, index, transform) encoded as a copy with a distance just
past the window, so the encoder could embed:
  - a few hundred of the most profitable words for web content, with
    their lengths and indexes: a few kB in total;
  - a small hash table on their first 4 bytes, constant or built at init;
  - the identity transform only, possibly "uppercase first" and "add a
    space".

These codes replace literals, i.e. the data with no earlier match in the
call, which is exactly what a stateless encoder lacks most, especially
at the beginning of each call. But they cost many bits: the distance is
about the window size, hence 14-16 extra bits with a 16-32 kB window
(the smallest window covering a call keeps them cheapest), plus the
command code: ~20-30 bits per word, vs ~6-7 bits per text-tree literal.
So only words of ~5-6 bytes or more pay off, and the subset should favour
long, frequent web words (function, return, document, stylesheet,
javascript, undefined, background, position, display:, ...), picked by
an offline tool on measured savings, like the text tree.

Not measured yet. The dictionary is available in brotli's sources (e.g.
lzbench's lz+entropy/brotli/common/dictionary.c) for a coverage test:
how many of the literal bytes left by slz -T are covered by dictionary
words of 6+ bytes.


4. Brotli vs slz in haproxy's regime (lzbench + zenc, 2026-11-08)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

hap-org.html (932561 bytes, 7 haproxy.org pages concatenated), each block
compressed independently. Brotli and zlib from lzbench -b<kB> -t1,1
(lzbench's own slz is 1.4-dev3 with the old level numbering, so slz
figures come from the current zenc instead: zenc -k <kB> -t -v, MB/s best
of 3 with -l 200). Xeon W-2145, taskset -c 4. The two harnesses differ
(lzbench in memory, zenc reading a mapped file), so compare speeds with
care; sizes are exact.

  block   brotli -0     brotli -1     brotli -2     zlib -1      zlib -6
  4 kB    37.60% 248    35.81% 176    34.75% 109    34.24%  94   32.14%  61
  16 kB   30.37% 251    29.73% 197    27.63% 156    29.01% 134   26.07%  63
  32 kB   28.72% 301    27.95% 198    25.60% 174    27.55% 150   24.17%  57
  1 MB    24.57% 490    22.71% 383    20.81% 207    25.86% 143   21.61%  45

  block   slz -1        slz -4        slz -T
  4 kB    45.58% 533    42.21% 459    35.35% 436
  16 kB   37.99% 607    34.85% 529    29.59% 504
  32 kB   36.06% 623    32.67% 555    27.96% 527
  1 MB    30.21% 565    30.11% 550    26.08% 519

(ratio, then compression MB/s)

Observations:
  - in haproxy's regime (independent 4-32 kB calls), slz -T already
    compresses HTML as well as brotli -0/-1, at 1.7-2.7x their speed:
    4 kB 35.35% vs 37.60/35.81, 16 kB 29.59% vs 30.37/29.73, 32 kB
    27.96% vs 28.72/27.95. The whole-file gap seen on 2026-11-01 comes
    from brotli's window across the file and its adaptive codes (at 1 MB
    brotli -1 is 13% smaller than slz -T), not from the format;
  - brotli -2 is 6.6% (16 kB) to 8.4% (32 kB) smaller than slz -T, at
    a third of its speed. q2 uses a better match finder; for comparison,
    FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH depth 2/4 gives -5.2%/-7.6% at 32 kB on this file
    with the fixed tree (../encoding/README, section 5). So much of q2's lead
    is likely the search rather than the format;
  - with the fixed tree, slz -4 is 17-19% and slz -1 28-29% larger than
    -T on HTML at 4-32 kB: the trained text tree is what puts slz at
    brotli -0/-1 level here.


5. Distance cache potential (SLZ_STATS, patch 0001)
---------------------------------------------------

Patch 0001 in this directory (originally commit 4648e7a) adds the counter
to SLZ_STATS; it applies on top of ../encoding/0001-0003.

SLZ_STATS simulates brotli's ring of the last 4 distances (initialized to
4, 11, 15, 16, shared by all calls of a stream) on the references slz
emits, and counts the short distance codes they would use. On a miss, it
also tries the 16 cached distances at the match position: if one gives
the same length, a cache-aware encoder would have used it ("switch",
counted as a hit, and the ring follows that choice); if one only gives a
shorter match (>= 4), it is counted apart.

% of references. code0 = last distance (incl. free), free = code 0 with
an implicit distance (insert <= 9, copy <= 69), c1-3 = 2nd-4th last,
c4-9 = last +-1..3, c10-15 = 2nd last +-1..3, hits = total incl. switch,
switch = switched alone, hitbits = fixed-tree distance bits of the hits
in bytes (what short codes would replace), short = misses with only a
shorter cached match.

  corpus  lvl k        totout      refs  code0    free    c1-3    c4-9  c10-15    hits  switch  hitbits   short
  hap-org  1   16      354239     64437  3.58%   3.07%   4.50%   2.90%   3.34%  14.33%   7.45%    14340   0.81%
  hap-org  1   32      336267     67698  3.52%   3.03%   4.22%   2.39%   3.16%  13.29%   7.61%    14653   0.80%
  hap-org  1   4096    281718     76071  3.26%   2.75%   4.19%   1.86%   2.90%  12.21%   7.39%    15733   0.79%
  hap-org  4   16      325039     71400  3.39%   2.92%   4.18%   2.75%   3.19%  13.50%   6.97%    14953   0.77%
  hap-org  4   32      304658     73567  3.32%   2.87%   4.26%   2.30%   3.03%  12.91%   7.23%    15377   0.79%
  hap-org  4   4096    280766     76105  3.29%   2.78%   4.21%   1.86%   2.90%  12.25%   7.43%    15803   0.79%
  hap-org  T   16      275939     71387  3.37%   2.90%   4.18%   2.75%   3.19%  13.49%   6.95%    14928   0.77%
