  silesia  4   32    96588868  17351141  8.71%   8.36%   7.99%   1.54%   1.97%  20.21%   8.46%  5073478   1.31%
  silesia  4   4096  91360519  18719547  7.77%   7.46%   7.44%   1.16%   1.81%  18.19%   8.41%  5137459   1.30%
  silesia  T   16    99931392  15268296  9.13%   8.79%   8.27%   1.96%   2.20%  21.57%   8.47%  4605822   1.35%
  silesia  T   32    95140431  16299687  8.56%   8.24%   7.94%   1.60%   2.06%  20.15%   8.53%  4755019   1.35%
  silesia  T   4096  88247695  18087642  8.00%   7.70%   7.57%   1.20%   1.87%  18.64%   8.61%  5081347   1.33%

(Without the switches, i.e. on slz's own choices only, hap-org -4 at
32 kB gives 6.20% hits, 1.00% on the last distance.)

Observations:
  - on the HTML only 12-14% of the references can use the cache, even
    when preferring cached distances on equal lengths; the last distance
    alone is 3.3%. HTML repeats structures, but not at constant
    distances. Half of the hits need the encoder to look for them;
  - the fixed-tree distance bits at stake are ~15 kB of a 260-305 kB
    output at 32 kB. With short codes costing ~3 bits (9.5k hits =
    3.6 kB), the net saving would be ~4.5% of the output (11.8 kB of
    260769 with -T), before any other effect of the format;
  - silesia does better (18-23% of references, mostly the last distance
    and the 2nd-4th ones) for a similar relative net saving (~5 MB of
    distance bits at stake on ~96 MB);
  - a cache-aware search costs up to 16 extra compares per reference
    (fewer if limited to codes 0-3).


6. Where this leaves the idea
-----------------------------

  - In haproxy's regime on HTML, the text tree already brings slz to
    brotli -0/-1 ratios at about twice their speed, so the motivation
    from the whole-file lzbench comparison does not hold there.
  - Measured potential of the format itself so far: the distance cache,
    ~4.5% of the output on HTML. Combined commands are mostly a CPU
    matter, within the 16-19% of the time spent encoding.
  - Unmeasured: the dictionary subset (the most promising for small
    calls, since it is the only history a stateless encoder can use) and
    literal context modeling (2-4 trees).
  - The rule of thumb set on 2026-11-01 still applies: if a brotli
    emitter only ends up 3-5% better than slz -T it's not worth it; 10%
    or more would be.

Next steps, in order:
  1. dictionary coverage: on the literals left by slz -T at 16/32 kB,
     count the bytes covered by dictionary words of 6+ bytes (identity
     transform), and estimate the net saving at ~20-30 bits per word;
  2. if 1 + the distance cache approach 10%: a prototype emitter in zenc
     fed by the existing match finder (one profile, NBLTYPES=1,
     NPOSTFIX=0, NDIRECT=0, one literal tree, static trained command and
     distance trees, distance cache from the start), validated with the
     reference decoder (brotli -d / python-brotli), per-call output
     checked against a margin like the chunkrt test;
  3. only then a minimal dictionary lookup and 2-4 literal contexts.


Status and conclusions as of 2026-11-08:
  - no real gain identified so far: in haproxy's regime the text tree
    already gives brotli -0/-1 ratios on HTML at about twice their speed,
    and the distance cache alone is worth ~4.5% of output. Only the
    dictionary subset (step 1 above) remains to be measured before
    dropping or pursuing the idea;
  - the match finder is not where brotli's better levels could help us.
    FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH with all candidates does save a lot of bytes (at
    32 kB with the fixed tree: -11.2% on hap-org.html, -8.4% on silesia),
    and brotli -2's 6.6-8.5% lead over slz -T is in that range, so it most
    likely comes from its search. But that search costs CPU we chose not
    to spend, and none of it comes from the format. Several modern
    implementations copy slz's matcher design, and lz4's own parse is no
    better than slz -4's (../lz4/README, section 2), which suggests that slz is
    close to optimal for its speed class.


7. Commands used
----------------

Build of zenc used for all slz figures (Xeon W-2145, so with the JCC
erratum padding; drop the two -Wa options on other machines):

  F="-O3 -g -fomit-frame-pointer -march=native -mno-avx512f \
     -Wa,-mbranches-within-32B-boundaries \
     -Wa,-malign-branch=jcc+fused+jmp+call -Isrc -DSLZ_VERSION=\"x\""
  gcc-15 $F -o zenc src/zenc.c src/slz_common.c src/slz.c
  gcc-15 $F -DSLZ_STATS=1 -o zenc-stats src/zenc.c src/slz_common.c src/slz.c

(make zenc USR_CFLAGS="-DSLZ_STATS=1" works as well.)

Section 4, brotli and zlib (lzbench at commit 32ab077, brotli 1.2.0,
zlib 1.3.2), -b<kB> compresses each block independently, -t1,1 sets the
minimum time of each measurement to 1 second:

  cd lzbench
  for b in 4 16 32 1024; do
      taskset -c 4 ./lzbench -b$b -t1,1 \
          -ebrotli,0,1,2/slz_deflate,1,2,3/zlib,1,6 /tmp/hap-org.html
  done

Section 4, slz sizes and speeds (-T is the text tree, run at level 3
internally whatever the level), best of 3 runs:

  for o in "-1" "-4" "-4 -T"; do
      for k in 4 16 32 1024; do
          taskset -c 4 ./zenc $o -k $k -l 200 -t -v /tmp/hap-org.html
      done
  done

zenc prints "totin=... totout=... ratio=...% ... <speed>MB/s saving ...";
the ratio and the first MB/s figure are used.

Section 5, distance cache (the ring is shared by all blocks of a zenc
run, i.e. one stream):

  for o in "-1" "-4" "-4 -T"; do
      for k in 16 32 4096; do
          ./zenc-stats $o -k $k -t -v /tmp/hap-org.html 2>&1 |
              sed -n '/brotli distance cache/,/shorter only/p'
      done
  done

The same with /dev/shm/silesia.tar for the silesia rows. The output of a
single run looks like this (hap-org.html, -4 -k 32):

  brotli distance cache (ring of the last 4 distances):
    last (code 0)             2442    3.32%, of which 2108 (2.87%) with an implicit distance
    2nd-4th (1-3)             3137    4.26%
    last +-1..3               1689    2.30%
    2nd +-1..3                2227    3.03%
    total hits                9495   12.91%, fixed tree distance bits on hits: avg 12.96, 15377 bytes total
      incl. switched          5319    7.23% (a cached distance gave the same length, counted above)
    shorter only               580    0.79%, would lose 13.38 bytes on avg
